{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Cox1964-Fig7-Al2O3-ZrO2-MgF2-AR",
  "Source": "J. T. Cox, G. Hass and J. B. Ramsey, \"Improved dielectric films for multilayer coatings and mirror protection,\" J. Phys. (Paris) 25, 250-254 (1964). doi:10.1051/jphys:01964002501-2025000 - Fig. 7 caption and text p. 253 (Al2O3 is the inside layer; ZrO2 n ~ 2.1 at 550 nm; deposited on glass at about 300 C)",
  "Notes": "Three-layer broadband antireflection coating lambda/4 - lambda/2 - lambda/4 of Al2O3 + ZrO2 + MgF2 on glass\nSpecification: Very broad region of low reflectance over the visible on glass ng = 1.51.\nReported: Text p. 253: very broad region of low reflectance; uncoated glass (ng = 1.51) shown for comparison. Fig. 7 (plot only, approximate): uncoated glass ~4.2 %; coated R below ~0.2 % from 400 to ~560 nm, rising to ~0.5 % at 620 nm, ~1 % at 650 nm and ~1.8 % at 700 nm.\nNotes: Caption lists 'lambda/4 - lambda/2 - lambda/4 of Al2O3 + ZrO2 + MgF2' from the glass outward (text: 'uses Al2O3 as the inside layer'); reversed here. Design wavelength not stated; 550 nm assumed (the wavelength at which the ZrO2 index is quoted). The plotted minimum region (400-560 nm) suggests a shorter design wavelength, perhaps ~500 nm. Al2O3 taken as the 300 C film (n = 1.63 at 500 nm) because the coating is deposited on glass at about 300 C. The paper gives no MgF2 index, so database MgF2 is used with a physical thickness of 550/(4 x 1.38) = 99.64 nm (assumes n = 1.38). Single glass surface, no back reflection.\nLayers from Fig. 7 caption and text p. 253 (Al2O3 is the inside layer; ZrO2 n ~ 2.1 at 550 nm; deposited on glass at about 300 C); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours R < 0.2 % from 453 to 697 nm; 0.222 % at 700 nm; paper R < ~0.2 % from 400 to ~560 nm, ~1.8 % at 700 nm (read off Fig. 7). The paper does not state the design wavelength; 550 nm is assumed, and the computed low-reflectance band reaches 700 nm while the plotted one rises from ~560 nm, so the paper’s design wavelength was probably lower (~500 nm).",
  "Wavelength": [
    400,
